menu "Orb networking"

    config ORB_NET_ENABLE
        bool "Bring up WiFi + the config-page HTTP server"
        default n
        help
            Phase 2 bring-up switch. When off, main/net is compiled but not
            started, so the clock runs with the pre-networking memory profile
            and the heap instrumentation can be compared either way.

            The point of enabling this on a development build is to collect the
            real internal-RAM numbers (orb_net_start() logs them at each stage);
            the WiFi + httpd budget is the one thing the plan could not settle
            without hardware.

    config ORB_NET_MEM_DEBUG
        bool "Log internal/DMA + PSRAM heap at each networking stage"
        depends on ORB_NET_ENABLE
        default y
        help
            Emits one line per stage: before the netif/event loops, after
            esp_wifi_init(), after esp_wifi_start(), on STA connect, and after
            httpd_start(). Compare the INTERNAL column against the ~105 KB
            budget before adopting any further trims.

    config ORB_NET_AP_FALLBACK
        bool "Start a SoftAP if there are no stored credentials"
        depends on ORB_NET_ENABLE
        default y
        help
            First-boot provisioning path: with no SSID saved, the orb comes up
            as an open "orb-xxxxxx" AP on channel 1 (channel 1 only, so it sits
            inside the 2.4 GHz-only radio; the 466x466 screen cannot show a QR
            code legibly, so the pairing URL is printed to the serial log and
            shown on-screen).

    config ORB_NET_AP_ISOLATED
        bool "Allow the persistent AP-only (isolated) mode"
        depends on ORB_NET_AP_FALLBACK
        default y
        help
            Lets an owner park a configured orb in a mode where it runs only its
            own open SoftAP and web page and never associates with any Wi-Fi
            network, so the ONLY way to reach it is that AP. The choice is
            persistent (NVS orb.net "mode" = "ap") and survives a reboot until a
            MODE command (serial door or the config page) switches it back to
            station. The on-demand rescue AP is separate and unaffected; this
            option only gates the deliberate, persistent isolated mode. A device
            in this mode is unreachable from the LAN and cannot be OTA'd over it:
            it is a self-contained clock serving 192.168.4.1.

    config ORB_NET_AP_HOLD_S
        int "Seconds to keep the SoftAP after the STA gets a lease"
        depends on ORB_NET_ENABLE
        default 120
        help
            The provisioning AP must not outlive its welcome: it is OPEN, and
            behind it sits an unauthenticated /api/v1/ota. The AP therefore
            always dies on a timer — but that timer is only the FALLBACK now:
            the confirmation hands the user a Copy / Open / Done choice and the
            AP closes the moment one of them is tapped (a POST to
            /api/v1/wifi/ap with an off body). It is deliberately NOT
            automatic when the message renders: Android binds its captive-
            portal webview to the AP, so closing the AP unasked took the
            page -- and the orb's brand-new address on it -- away with it.
            This value is the backstop for the path where nobody taps
            anything, counted from the lease landing, so a user reading the
            instructions has this whole window to act.

            0 closes the AP the instant the lease lands, before anyone can read
            the confirmation: the page renders and the webview goes with the AP.
            Leave this well above the time it takes to read the
            address and switch networks by hand (NOT recommended).

    config ORB_NET_AP_MANUAL_S
        int "Seconds an explicit POST /api/v1/wifi/ap keeps the SoftAP open"
        depends on ORB_NET_ENABLE
        default 900
        help
            Window for the on-demand path: a machine already on the LAN
            summons the AP with POST /api/v1/wifi/ap, and a phone at the wall
            joins it. Long enough to pair, join the AP and submit the form;
            short enough that a forgotten request cannot leave the AP open
            overnight. (The config page carries no button for this on
            purpose — anything that can reach the page can change the
            network from the page; see the h_wifi_ap comment.)

    config ORB_NET_AP_RESCUE_FAILS
        int "Failed associations before the SoftAP comes back up"
        depends on ORB_NET_ENABLE
        default 8
        help
            The recovery path for the case that has no other exit: credentials
            are stored but unusable (the passphrase on the router changed while
            the orb was powered off), so the boot path takes the station-only
            branch and no AP ever appears. After this many consecutive failed
            associations the orb opens the provisioning AP alongside the
            retries, and closes it again on a timer once it gets a lease.

            With the 5 s base back-off doubling to 120 s, 8 attempts is roughly
            8-9 minutes of failure — long enough that a router rebooting
            (1-3 min) never wakes the AP, short enough that a real lockout is
            fixable from a phone instead of with a USB cable and an NVS erase.
            Only the network saying no counts: a disconnect the orb caused
            itself (submitting a join, opening or closing the AP) spends no
            attempt, so pressing buttons can never summon a rescue AP by
            accumulation. Set 0 to disable (NOT recommended: it puts serial
            access back on the critical path for a changed Wi-Fi passphrase).


    config ORB_NET_HTTPD_PORT
        int "Config-server port"
        depends on ORB_NET_ENABLE
        default 80
        help
            Plain HTTP. TLS is terminated upstream (dev: DEV-LLM-STACK's
            Traefik with a real LE cert; production NAS-STACK: its own Traefik
            via the passthrough rules), so nothing here links mbedtls's
            server-side SSL path.

    config ORB_NET_PROV_SOJOURN_S
        int "Seconds a RECOVERY provisioning session stays open"
        depends on ORB_NET_AP_FALLBACK
        default 600
        help
            How long a provisioning boot that still holds usable credentials in
            flash keeps the AP up before giving up and rebooting into the normal
            STA path, so the stored network gets retried. This is the exit a
            recovery session needs and a factory device must NOT have: with no
            credentials stored, rebooting returns to the same provisioning
            branch, so the timer stays disarmed and the AP stays open
            indefinitely exactly as it does today. See the guard on
            sojourn_arms() and D4 in docs/orb-manager-plan.md.

            600 s is long enough to fumble a passphrase twice from a phone and
            short enough that an orb nobody is tending rejoins its own network
            within ten minutes rather than sitting in AP mode overnight. Set 0
            to disable the exit (not recommended: an unattended recovery session
            then holds an OPEN AP with an unauthenticated /api/v1/ota behind it
            until someone power-cycles the device).

    config ORB_NET_MDNS
        bool "Advertise _orb._tcp over mDNS so the hub can browse for orbs"
        depends on ORB_NET_ENABLE
        default y
        help
            The hub finds orbs by browsing for the _orb._tcp service instead of
            sweeping the subnet or being told an address (see
            docs/orb-manager-plan.md section 5). This answers PTR/SRV/TXT/A for
            one instance named from the chip's BASE MAC, on the STA interface
            and, while the SoftAP is open, on that one too — which is the only
            way a factory-reset orb with no lease can be found at all.

            One UDP socket carries both joins (two IP_ADD_MEMBERSHIP calls),
            because LWIP_MAX_SOCKETS is 10 and six of them belong to httpd's
            floor. Hand-rolled rather than the espressif/mdns component for the
            same margin reason: that one allocates socket pairs per interface.

            The announcement is an index, not an authority: anyone on the LAN
            can forge these bytes, so the hub still dials /api/v1/status and
            compares the mac it is told against the mac in the TXT (item 6's
            cross-lock) before it trusts an address.

            Turn off to reclaim one socket and a 3 KB task stack out of internal
            DRAM; every other path — page, API, radio — is unaffected. Nothing
            else in the firmware depends on it.

    config ORB_NET_CAPTIVE_DNS
        bool "Answer the AP's DNS so phones pop 'Sign in to network'"
        depends on ORB_NET_ENABLE
        default y
        help
            A phone that joins the provisioning AP decides what to show its user
            by resolving the reachability-check hostnames its OS probes for that
            purpose. The default softAP advertises no resolver at all, so those
            probes fail silently and the phone reports "no internet" (and may drop
            the AP) instead of the "Sign in to network" prompt that would open the
            config page. With this on, the AP advertises itself as the resolver and
            answers every query with its own address (192.168.4.1), so the existing
            /generate_204 + /hotspot-detect.html redirects fire and the browser
            lands on the page without the user typing an IP.

            The responder binds the AP address only, never 0.0.0.0, so the
            station's own DNS — including SNTP — is never redirected to the orb.
            It starts and stops with the SoftAP itself. Turn off only to reclaim
            the small task + one UDP socket; the config page stays reachable at
            the printed http://192.168.4.1/ either way.

    config ORB_DEBUG_HTTP
        bool "Serve /api/v1/screen (synthetic knob + screen switch) to the LAN"
        depends on ORB_NET_ENABLE
        default y
        help
            The bench door: the rotary encoder is three GPIOs driven only by a
            finger, so without GET /api/v1/screen?to=&turn=&push= the menu and the
            picker cannot be exercised from a script, and a screen nobody has
            painted has never been looked at. It switches what the glass shows and
            injects knob events; it reaches no card, radio or bootloader path.

            It is still a door anybody on the LAN can knock, and it is the only
            route whose whole reason for existing is measurement. Turn off for a
            build that leaves for a shelf, and prove the build actually dropped it
            (this file's sdkconfig caveat): a route compiled out answers 404, and
            every scripted walk that drives it fails with nothing in the log.

endmenu

menu "Orb microSD (theme storage)"

    config ORB_SD_ENABLE
        bool "Mount the microSD card (1-bit SPI) at /sd"
        default n
        help
            Brings up the TF socket on the Waveshare board as SDSPI on
            SPI3_HOST (GPIO 1/2/3, CS 41) with FATFS, and creates /sd/themes.
            This is the theme import path; the dial NEVER reads art from here
            while running (1-bit SPI caps at ~1.5 MB/s — see
            docs/usb-sd-msc-plan.md §9 and main/sd/orb_sd.h).

            Off means the socket is not probed at all and esp_vfs_fat, sdmmc
            and esp_driver_sdspi drop out of the image, which is what makes it
            usable as the control side of a frame-time A/B.

    config ORB_SD_SPI_MAX_KHZ
        int "Highest SPI clock to try first, in kHz"
        depends on ORB_SD_ENABLE
        default 20000
        help
            The card is driven at this clock first and falls back to 10 MHz
            then 4 MHz if the data phase fails, exactly like the Arduino
            original (orb-os/src/sdcard.cpp). The fallback matters: an SDSC
            card powers up, answers the init commands at the 400 kHz probe
            clock and then fails the data phase at 20 MHz with no hint why —
            without the ladder that card is reported as absent.

    config ORB_SD_RETRY_S
        int "Seconds to wait before re-probing an absent card"
        depends on ORB_SD_ENABLE
        default 30
        help
            The socket has no card-detect line, so presence is discovered by
            talking to it, and a failed attempt costs the command timeout at
            every clock rate in the ladder. The theme page lists /sd while a
            person browses it; without this back-off an empty socket is
            re-probed on every request, and a card inserted mid-life is only
            noticed at the next reboot.


endmenu

menu "Orb idle dim"

    config ORB_IDLE_DIM_ENABLE
        bool "Dim the dial after a period without a touch"
        default y
        help
            The dial is a clock, so it is on the wall and unattended far more
            than it is being read. After the timeout below with no detent, tap
            or motion, the face drops to ORB_IDLE_DIM_PCT; the next touch returns
            it to the recorded user level rather than to a hard-wired 100, so the
            dim ducks from and returns to wherever the brightness control left
            it. That level is the boot 100% until a brightness control exists —
            idle.lit_pct on /api/v1/status reports it as it really is.

            This is the BUILD switch. The dial's MENU -> DISPLAY also carries a
            runtime switch, persisted to NVS; the saved choice wins on every
            boot, and this option is what a factory-fresh orb (or one whose NVS
            holds nothing) starts with. Turning the runtime switch off leaves
            this task ticking but never dimming; turning this option off drops
            the whole feature from the image.

            Off leaves the panel exactly where its own init left it: the idle
            task is never created and register 0x51 is never written.

    config ORB_IDLE_DIM_TIMEOUT_S
        int "Seconds without a touch before the face dims"
        depends on ORB_IDLE_DIM_ENABLE
        default 600
        help
            Ten minutes, the owner's number, and the boot default: the dial's
            MENU -> DISPLAY timeout picker overrides it at runtime and the
            chosen value is persisted to NVS, winning on every boot after the
            first change. Anything a hand can do counts:
            turning the knob, pressing it, or moving the orb. Watch the countdown
            rather than a stopwatch -- idle.since_s climbs to idle.timeout_s on
            /api/v1/status (the EFFECTIVE value, not this one) and idle.dimmed
            flips when it lands, and
            tools/idle_watch.py does exactly that, injecting the wake detent for
            the half no log line can speak for. Set low only for the bench -- at
            60 s the dim becomes the visible state and a long screenshot looks
            like a broken display. The DISPLAY picker's 1 min entry usually
            serves the same bench purpose without a rebuild.

    config ORB_IDLE_DIM_PCT
        int "Brightness to drop to while idle, percent"
        depends on ORB_IDLE_DIM_ENABLE
        default 20
        range 1 100
        help
            Written straight to the CO5300's 0x51 (display brightness) register
            through the vendor driver, which maps 0..100 onto 0..255. Kept above
            zero deliberately: an AMOLED that reads as off is indistinguishable
            from an AMOLED that has died, and the dial must stay findable in a
            dark room.

    config ORB_APP_POWER_MAX_S
        int "Longest dial-awake an APP may request, seconds"
        depends on ORB_IDLE_DIM_ENABLE
        default 300
        range 5 3600
        help
            The ceiling on the broker's power_request entry (plan §4.2):
            an app is a third input that can only ASK — the Kconfig values
            here are the boot defaults, the owner's DISPLAY choice is the
            truth, and this number is the most wall-clock an app may ask
            the countdown to stretch to in one request. A longer request
            is clamped to it, not refused, because the row that said
            "keep reading" should still keep reading, just not forever.
            The hold form bypasses the number entirely and is why the
            capability is owner-granted on the dial and auto-revoked the
            moment the app leaves the glass (orb_svc_app_suspended).

    config ORB_IDLE_MOTION_ENABLE
        bool "Wake on the accelerometer as well as the rotary"
        depends on ORB_IDLE_DIM_ENABLE
        default y
        help
            Brings up the board's QMI8658 over I2C (GPIO 15/14) and polls its
            accelerometer for real motion: a pick-up, a knock on the desk, the
            case shifting. Only a dimmed face acts on it; a lit one samples once
            a second to keep the deviation high-water current, because that is
            the figure the threshold below is set from and the orb is lit while
            somebody is shaking it.

            Off leaves the chip unaddressed and the driver unreferenced, so the
            linker's section gc drops it from the image — which is what makes
            this the control side of a current-draw A/B — and leaves the rotary
            as the only wake source. Confirm it on the map file rather than
            trusting the claim: imu_read_accel should be absent from
            build/*.map when this is off and present when it is on.

    config ORB_IDLE_MOTION_SAMPLES
        int "Consecutive motion samples required to wake"
        depends on ORB_IDLE_MOTION_ENABLE
        default 3
        range 1 20
        help
            How many tripping samples in a row the dimmed poll (20 Hz) must see
            before it credits the accelerometer with a wake.

            This exists because of what the dial does on a real desk: an orb left
            untouched dimmed itself correctly and lifted again 3.0 s later, and
            again 26.6 s later on the next cycle, both times on a single ~55 mg
            spike on the gravity axis and with nobody near it. The dark poll is
            20 Hz precisely so a knock tens of milliseconds long is not missed,
            and that same rate lands on one-sample vibration the 1 Hz lit poll
            almost never sees — which is why the same still orb reads a 7 mg
            high-water lit and 62 mg within seconds of the dark.

            Requiring a run separates those without spending sensitivity: a held
            tilt reads motion on 12 consecutive samples at 160 mg and 19 at
            400 mg, so a hand lifting the orb clears three easily while a desk
            jolt never does. tools/host_idle_dim_test.py pins the run logic.

            1 restores the old pass-through behaviour, which is the setting to
            A/B against if a wake ever seems to be missed: raise this back to 3
            only after confirming the missed wake was not simply this filter.

    config ORB_IDLE_MOTION_MG
        int "Motion threshold, milli-g"
        depends on ORB_IDLE_MOTION_ENABLE
        default 40
        range 5 500
        help
            The deviation from the learned rest vector that counts as motion, on
            any one axis. The detector follows the baseline while the orb is
            still, so a slow tilt or an orb carried to another room re-baselines
            instead of holding the face awake forever — which is the failure
            mode a fixed magnitude test has.

            40 mg is a starting point, NOT a measured number: it clears the
            sensor's own quantisation step (4096 LSB per g at the ±8 g range
            imu.c sets, so one LSB is a quarter mg) by a wide margin while
            staying small enough to catch a hand lifting the orb.

            Measure it rather than guessing, from the two idle.worst_* fields on
            /api/v1/status — the widest deviation from the learned rest vector the
            accelerometer has reported since boot, kept apart by which poll saw it:
              1. Prop the orb on its final surface and let it dim (or set
                 ORB_IDLE_DIM_TIMEOUT_S low for the bench). Read
                 idle.worst_dark_mg: the dimmed poll is the one that competes with
                 this threshold, and the lit figure understates it — measured on
                 this bench, 7 mg lit against 62 mg dark, from single-sample desk
                 jolt spikes at ~55 mg on the gravity axis, roughly every 20-30 s.
              2. Lift the orb as a person would, read it again. The threshold must
                 sit below that.
              3. Set it between, nearer the floor than the lift. If the floor and
                 the lift overlap, the floor is the desk talking: move the orb or
                 raise ORB_IDLE_MOTION_SAMPLES rather than pushing the threshold
                 above a real pick-up, which would leave the dial dimmed on a
                 wall nobody can wake.
            idle.wakes says how often motion alone ended a dim: a count climbing
            with nobody near the orb means the threshold is under the floor, and a
            count stuck at zero through deliberate lifts means it is over the lift.
            tools/host_idle_dim_test.py pins the decision's logic, the high-water
            and the run-of-N; only the orb can pin its threshold.

endmenu
